<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n45979" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sublŭvĭes</span></b> , em,
		e, f.
		subluo, that which is washed off, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>filth</i>, <i>dirt</i> (post-Aug.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> In gen.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">via subluvie caenosā lubrica,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">App. M. 9, p. 221, 2</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">limosa,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Amm. 15, 4</span></i>
			.— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In
			partic., <i>a disease in the feet of sheep</i>, <i>the foul</i>, <i><span class="en">Col. 7, 5,
				11</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">so in the feet of men,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					30, 9, 23, § 80</span></i>
			.—Called also <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sublŭvĭum</span></b> , ii,
				n., <i><span class="en">Marc. Emp. 18 <i>fin.</i></span></i></div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
